  "course_key": "g3-sec2-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec2-g2",
  "position": 4,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Enlargement and reduction of a plane figure",
  "summary": "Apply enlargement and reduction to lengths, coordinates, perimeter and area.",
  "outcomes": [
    "2.4"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g2-lesson-03"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Enlarge coordinates about a stated centre.",
    "Distinguish linear and area scale factors.",
    "Reverse an enlargement to recover original measurements."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g2-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-24-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For an enlargement with centre C and factor k, the image point lies on the line through C and the original point. Its directed displacement from C is multiplied by k."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "P'=C+k(P-C)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-04-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An enlargement with centre C and scale factor k maps each point A along the line CA, so that the distance from C to the image A′ is k times the distance from C to A. The diagram shows the centre C(2, −2), the point A(6, 4) and the line joining them, used in the second worked example; the image lies on this line. On a coordinate grid, A′ = C + k(A − C). Factors greater than 1 enlarge, and factors between 0 and 1 reduce."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g2-lesson-04-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-04-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "About the origin, multiply both coordinates by k. About another centre, subtract the centre coordinates, scale the displacement, then add the centre back. Lengths are multiplied by k and areas by k². The centre, the object point and the image point must lie on one straight line."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-24-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: enlarge about the origin",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Point P = (2, 3) is enlarged about the origin by scale factor 4. Find P′."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "About the origin, multiply each coordinate by the scale factor."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "P'=(4\\times2,\\ 4\\times3)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "P'=(8,12)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "P′ is on the ray from O through P: (8, 12) = 4 × (2, 3), and it is 4 times as far from O."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-04-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: enlarge about another centre",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the diagram, point A = (6, 4) is enlarged by scale factor 1/2 about C = (2, −2). Find the image A′."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the displacement from C to A."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A-C=(6-2,\\ 4-(-2))=(4,6)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Scale the displacement."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\tfrac12(4,6)=(2,3)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Add C back."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A'=(2,-2)+(2,3)=(4,1)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "A′ is halfway from C to A, so C, A′ and A lie on the line in the diagram."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-04-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not multiply the coordinates directly unless the centre is the origin. Scale both horizontal and vertical displacements. An area factor is the square of the linear factor, even for a reduction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-24-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 15 cm side is reduced by scale factor 2/5. Find the length of the image side, in cm."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "6",
        "canonical_latex": "6",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Multiply the original length by the scale factor: 15 × 2/5 = 6 cm."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-04-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Enlargement with centre C and scale factor k: A′ = C + k(A − C), and C, A and A′ lie on one line. About the origin, multiply both coordinates by k."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Lengths are multiplied by k and areas by k². Factors between 0 and 1 give reductions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
